  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a clutch plate?",
      "a": "The clutch plate is a disc faced with high-friction material, joined to the gearbox shaft. When it is squeezed against the spinning flywheel, friction makes it turn too.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a friction plate?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What do the clutch springs do?",
      "a": "The springs press the pressure plate hard against the clutch plate, clamping it to the flywheel. Without the pedal pressed, the clutch is always engaged.",
      "asks": [
        "What holds a clutch together?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do you press the clutch to change gear?",
      "a": "Pressing the pedal disconnects the engine from the gearbox, so no turning force passes through while the gears are swapped. Otherwise the gears would grind.",
      "asks": [
        "What happens when you press the clutch?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a clutch slip?",
      "a": "When the plate is only lightly pressed, it slides against the flywheel while being dragged round. This slipping lets a car move off smoothly, but too much wears the plate and makes heat.",
      "asks": [
        "What is clutch slip?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Do other machines have clutches?",
      "a": "Yes. Motorbikes, lawnmowers, some drills and cordless screwdrivers have clutches. A screwdriver's clutch slips at a set force so it does not drive screws too far.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does a drill have a clutch?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "A friction clutch transmits turning force by clamping a friction disc between two spinning surfaces.",
    "A car's clutch is engaged when the pedal is up and disengaged when it is pressed down."
  ],
